{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>If his master, etc.</b> It is difficult: why did He begin speaking in the second person — ״כִּי תִקְנֶה״ — and in the midst of speech reverted to speaking in the third person: ״אִם אֲדֹנָיו יִתֶּן לוֹ״, ״תִּהְיֶה לַאדֹנֶיהָ״, ״וְהִגִּישׁוֹ אֲדֹנָיו״ — the entire passage in the third person? It would have been proper to complete the speech in the manner it began: ״אִם תִּתֵּן לוֹ״, ״תִּהְיֶה לְךָ״, etc., ״וְהִגַּשְׁתָּ אוֹתוֹ״. And one might say that He did not attribute the match-making to the master following what they said, of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 68), that the Holy One, blessed be He, makes matches. Therefore He said <b>if his master</b> — the true Master, for to Him the children of Israel are servants — gives him a wife. But this explanation is not adequate, for He said ״the woman and her children shall be her master's.״ Further, one needs to know why it was necessary to say <b>and he shall go out by himself</b> — is it not self-evident that he goes out by himself from His saying ״the woman and her children shall be her master's״? Further, one needs to know why He doubled the expression <b>if he says, he shall say</b>:" "<b>Indeed,</b> the intent of the verses is this: because El decreed a modification of the law ״if in his body he came, in his body he shall go out״ — meaning that the master may not give him a Canaanite maidservant unless he was already married, as they said in Kiddushin (Kiddushin 20a). And one might ask: why did HaShem decree this modification of the law — what difference does it make whether he was married or not? Therefore the verse comes with wisdom to give a reason, and states that the reason is <b>if his master gives him a wife</b> — who is a Canaanite maidservant, as is understood from the context — the rule is that the woman and her children shall be her master's, while he, the slave, goes out by himself, meaning, without any wife at all. And since this is so, if his master gives him a wife and he has been unmarried all along, he will not want to leave servitude for two reasons: first, love of his wife and children; and second, that upon leaving he remains unmarried — so there are two evils in his departure. Given this, every male slave would not want to leave his master — which is not what HaShem, blessed be He, desires. Therefore He decreed that the master may not give him a wife unless he is already married, for in this way the separation from his wife and children is lightened, since he has a free Israelite wife, and leaving will seem easy in his eyes. And according to the verse ״if his master,״ etc., HaShem intended, in the order of the matters, to give a reason with pleasing language for why He altered the law." "And His saying <b>and if he says, he shall say</b> — meaning, besides the one utterance that HaShem made providing a remedy as mentioned, He further says <b>I love</b> etc., <b>and his master shall bring him</b> etc., <b>and he shall bore</b> etc. — for this testifies that he does not care about the yoke of HaShem, blessed be He, and acquires a master for himself:" "<b>Further,</b> it appears fitting to explain the passage by way of remez (hint), for the Torah commands man to set continually before his eyes all these matters, and that is His saying <b>which you shall set before them</b> — that man should always place this matter before him. And that is <b>when you acquire a Hebrew slave</b>. Do you not know that man is divided into two: one part spiritual, and it is the essence; and one part physical, which HaShem created for the soul to serve it, through which he fulfills many commandments that HaShem commanded. And it is called by the name עֶבֶד (slave), for it is nothing but a servant to the soul, for whose sake man is called אָדָם, as the verse says (Exodus 30:32) ״upon the flesh of man, not,״ etc., which indicates that the flesh is not called אָדָם, and likewise He said (Ezekiel 34:31) ״you are Adam״ — you are called Adam and not the nations of the world (Bava Metzia 114a), for they have no spiritual soul. And therefore the Torah commands man <b>when you acquire a Hebrew slave</b> — meaning עִבְרִי — one who passes (עוֹבֵר), for he does not endure forever, for what man lives and does not see death, after the manner of (Psalms 144:4) ״like a passing shadow.״ And the time of his fixed duration is until sixty years, after the manner of His saying (Job 5:26) ״you shall come to the grave in full vigor (בְכֶלַח),״ and they said in the Talmud (Mo'ed Katan 28a) that at sixty years is a man's time to go, according to the calculation of בְּכֶלַח, for then he is like a sheaf ascending. And this is His saying <b>six years</b> — meaning, all his days are sixty, and although He said six, there is a reckoning that includes one totality, and here it includes the tenth. <b>And in the seventh</b> — meaning, in the seventh decade the slave shall go out to freedom, after the manner of His saying (Psalms 88:6) ״among the dead, free.״ And His saying the word <b>חִנָּם (for nothing)</b> alludes by whose hand the departure will come — through חִנָּם, which is Samael and his hosts, who are alluded to in the word חִנָּם, as they said in the holy Zohar (Zohar II:128b) that with words of מַגָּנָא (manna) they sustain that other side, and therefore they have no dominion over a matter of a commandment that a person does with precious blood (i.e., at great cost). And likewise you will find that every matter of impurity comes חִנָּם (for nothing, effortlessly), whereas a matter of commandment and holiness requires great exertion and precious blood." "<b>If in his body he came.</b> One must examine why He used this expression, and did not use the customary expression in the Holy Tongue ״if alone,״ as in His saying (Genesis 32:25) ״and Jacob was left alone.״" "<b>However,</b> one must know that if one attunes oneself to commandments and good deeds and acquires power for oneself, one will find the strength to rise on the day of resurrection, in the manner of the verse (Psalms 72:16): ״וְיָצִיצוּ מֵעִיר כְּעֵשֶׂב הָאָרֶץ״ (Ketubot 111a). And this is what it says here <b>if by himself</b> — meaning the expression גף (wing), in the manner of the verse (Proverbs 9:3): ״עַל גַּפֵּי מְרוֹמֵי״ — which alludes to the commandments and good deeds, as they said, may their memory be for blessing (Shabbat 49a): ״Israel is compared to a dove״ and so forth. And its saying <b>he shall come</b> — meaning in the manner of Onkelos (Genesis 28:21): ״כִּי בָא הַשֶּׁמֶשׁ״ — עָאל שִׁמְשָׁא. <b>by himself he shall go out</b> — meaning with that wing he shall go out from the earth at the time of resurrection, and after his falling, resurrection shall be his. And knowledge of the opposites is equal: if in this world he does not strive to acquire the precious remedy, he will have no strength to rise. And this is their statement, may their memory be for blessing (Ketubot 111b), on the verse (Proverbs 3:18): ״עֵץ חַיִּים״ and so forth — that the bodies of the peoples of the earth do not endure except for those who hold fast to it." "And its saying <b>if he is the husband of a woman, then his wife shall go out with him</b> — here the Torah alluded to a great sod, which is that there is a person who acquires his soul through the power of his good deeds and becomes the husband of the holy soul. And you must know what they said in the Zohar (vol. 3, 91a) regarding the verse ״שׁוֹר אוֹ כֶשֶׂב אוֹ עֵז כִּי יִוָּלֵד״ and so forth — that animals, from the time of their birth, acquire the intellect that the ox possesses; not so the human — according to his deeds he merits, they give him a nefesh; if he merits more, and so forth; if he merits more, and so forth — up to here. See then that through his deeds he acquires the soul called אִשָּׁה (woman), as we also find that the sages of truth called it so (Tikkun 6). Therefore it says <b>if he is the husband of a woman</b> — that he acquired her in the manner described, <b>and his wife shall go out</b> — meaning even after his wife's departure, even so she is still with him and he remains alive even after his death. Go out and learn from the incident brought in tractate Shabbat (Shabbat 152b), of those people of Kefar and so forth — a man was fragrant among them and so forth — who would speak after his death. And this is their statement, may their memory be for blessing (Berakhot 18a): the righteous, even in their death, are called living." "<b>Furthermore,</b> it intends in its saying <b>if he is the husband of a woman</b>, to say: if he is a person all of whose thoughts and efforts and toil in this world, and even his food and drink, are only for the sake of the soul, in the manner of the verse (Proverbs 13:25): ״צַדִּיק אוֹכֵל לְשֹׂבַע נַפְשׁוֹ״ — to amplify the spirituality — such a person may be called a husband of a woman, like a husband who toils for his wife to feed and sustain and maintain her and to fulfill her desire; so too this man does for his soul. A person like this, whose material has been refined and has become of the nature of the form in this existence — his soul can endure to dwell with him in his presence even after her departure." "And its saying <b>if his master gives him a wife</b> and so forth — meaning, if he is not a husband of a woman in the manner stated, but a wife was given to him from Heaven. For you will find many people who merit a pure soul through the sod of the union at the time of formation on account of his righteous father. <b>And she bears him sons or daughters</b> — its meaning is in the manner of their statement (Genesis Rabbah ch. 30): the primary offspring of the righteous and so forth; also, that through good deeds a good angel will be born, as they said, may their memory be for blessing (Avot ch. 4): one who performs one commandment acquires for himself one advocate, and so forth; and so it is explicitly explained in the holy Zohar. Therefore it says <b>and she bears him sons or daughters</b> — the aspect of sons consists of commandments performed with great intensity, and the aspect of daughters is a level below that of sons. Such a person — <b>the woman and her children shall be her master's</b> — to negate what the man who acquired the woman through his intensification merited, having become the husband of the woman so that she would be with him always; not so in this case, but rather <b>they shall be her master's</b> — for such a person did not acquire her to be her husband, whereby she would not separate from him. Also, for a second interpretation: this one did not refine his body sufficiently to be worthy that the soul should not separate from him, and therefore <b>they shall be her master's</b>. <b>And he shall go out</b> and so forth — meaning, at the time of his departure from the grave at the time of resurrection, <b>he shall go out by himself</b> — meaning, the merits that he has; and the resurrection shall not be taken from him due to the absence of the matter stated." "And its saying <b>but if the servant shall say</b> and so forth — here the verse informs us of the service of the Israelite servant who is inflamed with passion for the service of his Master; and even after his powers are spent, he intensely desires to serve the Master. And this is its saying <b>I love my master, my wife, and my children</b> — they are the soul and the commandments that he performs in this world — and he does not wish to leave this world to freedom like the dead; and this indicates the passion and desire for the Master. To such a person the Master promises that he shall be called the servant of YHWH, and He will fulfill his desire — the desire of his heart He has given him; but not now, for now he has nothing more in the world except <b>and he shall serve him forever</b> — in the world to come, YHWH will choose him from among the angels to be His faithful servant. And we find that so it was with Moses, whom YHWH called (Deuteronomy 34:5) עֶבֶד ה׳ (servant of YHWH), for he longed to live, to enter the land of Israel to serve YHWH and fulfill His commandments (Sotah 14a). And what the verse says <b>and he shall pierce</b> and so forth <b>with the awl</b> — you will find in gematria that מַרְצֵעַ equals four hundred worlds of silver, as written in the Zohar (vol. 1, 123a). And its saying <b>and he shall serve him forever</b> — meaning, it shall be reckoned concerning him as though he lived all the days of the world and served in it the holy service — a good intention, and so forth. <b>Furthermore,</b> it alluded in <b>forever</b> a sod known to those who know holy knowledge."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {3 "The reading of עֲשִׂירִי as עֲשִׂירִית — 'the seventh decade' — is adopted on the assumption that Or HaChaim plays on the numeric value or ordinal sense of the word; the Hebrew is unusual and the precise intent may involve a further wordplay."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:32:16.207771402Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}